dash: a manifest read leaves libxml2's global state to whoever else uses it

The reader called xmlCleanupParser() after every manifest it parsed,
the first one and each a live manifest is fetched again for. That
function frees libxml2's global state: its dictionaries' lock, the key
each thread's state is kept under, the once-only initialisation. Its own
documentation says it is not thread-safe, that memory corruption is
likely when any other thread may call into libxml2, and that it is for
the moment before a process exits.

A player that plays two DASH sources at once, or one while another is
looked at, has two demuxers in two threads, each reading a manifest
when its turn comes. One of them tearing down what the other is using
is a crash that depends on timing. Since 2.9.11 libxml2 cleans up after
itself when the process ends, so nothing is leaked by not calling it.

--- a/libavformat/dashdec.c	2026-11-15 20:33:48
+++ b/libavformat/dashdec.c	2026-11-15 20:33:48
@@ -1431,7 +1431,9 @@
 cleanup:
         /*free the document */
         xmlFreeDoc(doc);
-        xmlCleanupParser();
+        /* libxml2's global state is left as it is: other demuxers, in
+         * other threads, may be reading a manifest at this moment, and
+         * libxml2 frees its own when the process exits. */
         xmlFreeNode(mpd_baseurl_node);
     }
 
